{
  "abstract": "Partial Poisson summation of a binary cubic preserves a short shift but leaves a signed modulus sum. We bound two complete contributions to this sum over the Eisenstein integers. For modulus norm $Q$, nonzero Fourier frequencies of norm at most $W\\le Q$, divisor-bounded coefficients, a squarefree moving twist $d$, and all fixed primary ray sectors modulo $18$, the auxiliary Kubota polar contribution is\n$$\n\\ll_\\epsilon (QWNdND)^\\epsilon\\left(WQ^{1/3}+(Nd)^{-1/6}Q^{5/6}\\sqrt W\\right).\n$$\nThe proof averages the explicit cubic theta residue along $d(x^3+1)$, retains oscillation in the modulus profile, and includes singular polynomial values. An exact local calculation identifies the puncture density. We also bound the entire zero dual frequency after Cauchy in the Fourier variable by $WQ^{1+\\epsilon}$, including all prime powers and overlaps. The specified nonunit reconstruction gives physical polar size $M^{3+\\epsilon}/R$ at $Q\\asymp M^3$, saving over $M^4/R^2$ for $R\\le M^{1-\\eta}$. Finally, affine-mean centering removes an exactly annihilated component before a sparse spectral moment, whose required estimate is stated and remains unproved. Reproducible CPU calculations check arithmetic conventions and evaluate one auxiliary polar packet; they show substantial remaining coherence at that scale without fitting an asymptotic exponent. The signed regular contribution is not controlled, and no new zero-free region or resolution of RH follows.",
